Sure! I'll add a try-catch block in the `scheduleDeployment` function to handle errors gracefully. Here's the updated code:

```javascript
// src/scheduler.js
async function scheduleDeployment(deploymentId) {
    try {
        const result = await startDeployment(deploymentId);
        console.log('Deployment successful:', result);
    } catch (error) {
        errorHandler.handleDeploymentError(deploymentId, error);
    }
}
```

Additionally, I will create an error handling utility:

```javascript
// src/utils/errorHandler.js
module.exports.handleDeploymentError = (deploymentId, error) => {
    console.error(`Error occurred during deployment ${deploymentId}:`, error);
    // Further error handling logic can be implemented here
};
```

